","To calculate the change in entropy (ΔS) for the reaction, we need to use the standard molar entropy values (S°) of the reactants and products. The reaction is:

3 moles of CO(g) + 3 moles of O2(g) → 3 moles of CO2(g)

First, we need the standard molar entropy values for CO, O2, and CO2 at 298 K. These values can be found in thermodynamic tables or thermodynamic tables in the reference book. The standard molar entropies are:

S°(CO) = 195.6 J/mol·K
S°(O2) = 205.1 J/mol·K
S°(CO2) = 213.6 J/mol·K

Now, we can calculate the change in entropy (ΔS) using the formula:

ΔS = ΣnS°(products) - ΣnS°(reactants)

where n is the number of moles of each substance.

ΔS = [3 * S°(CO2)] - [3 * S°(CO) + 3 * S°(O2)]

ΔS = [3 * 213.6 J/mol·K] - [3 * 195.6 J/mol·K + 3 * 205.1 J/mol·K]

ΔS = 640.8 J/mol·K - [586.8 J/mol·K + 615.3 J/mol·K]

ΔS = 640.8 J/mol·K - 1202.1 J/mol·K

ΔS = -561.3 J/mol·K

The change in entropy for the reaction is -561.3 J/mol·K.",relevance,"# Task Description
