","The concentration of hydronium ions \([H_3O^+]\) is given as \(10^{-4}\) M. To determine the nature of the solution, we compare \([H_3O^+]\) to the concentration of hydroxide ions \([OH^-]\). In a neutral solution, \([H_3O^+] = [OH^-] = 10^{-7}\) M. The relationship between \([H_3O^+]\) and \([OH^-]\) is given by the ion product constant \(K_w\):
\[
[H_3O^+][OH^-] = K_w = 10^{-14} \text{ M}^2
\]
For the given solution, \([H_3O^+] = 10^{-4}\) M. Substituting into the equation:
\[
10^{-4} \times [OH^-] = 10^{-14} \text{ M}^2
\]
\[
