\[
\text{Distance} = \text{Speed} \times \text{Time} = 80 \text{ steps/min} \times 20 \text{ min} \times 65 \text{ cm/step} = 1600 \times 65 \text{ cm} = 104000 \text{ cm}.
\]
Converting to meters:
\[
104000 \text{ cm} = \frac{104000}{100} \text{ m} = 1040 \text{ m}.
\]
Now, we find the time it takes Lily to reach the same destination. Laura runs at 90 steps per minute, each 55 cm long. 
The speed of Lila is:
\[
\text{Speed} = 90 \text{ steps/min} \times 55 \text{ cm/step} = 4950 \text{ cm/min}.
\]
Converting to meters per minute:
\[
4950 \text{ cm/min} = \frac{4950}{100} \text{ m/min} = 49.5 \text{ m/min}.
\]
The distance is 1040 m, so the time it takes Lily to reach the destination is:
\[
\text{Time} = \frac{\text{Distance}}{\text{Speed}} = \frac{1040 \text{ m}}{49.5 \text{ m/min}} = \frac{1040}{49.5} \text{ min} = \frac{10400}{495} \text{ min} = \frac{2080}{99} \text{ min}.
\]
\[
\frac{2080}{99} \approx 21 \text{ min}.
\]
Thus, the time it takes Lila to reach the destination is \(\boxed{21 \text{minutes}}\).
