","To find the frequency domain representation of the function \( f(t) = 3\sin(2\pi t) + 2\cos(4\pi t) \), we use the Fourier transform formula:
\[
F(f) = \frac{1}{2\pi} \int_{-\infty}^{\infty} f(t) e^{-i2\pi ft} \, dt
\]
Substituting \( f(t) \), we get:
\[
F(f) = \frac{1}{2\pi} \int_{-\infty}^{\infty} (3\sin(2\pi t) + 2\cos(4\pi t)) e^{-i2\pi ft} \, dt
\]
This can be separated into two integrals:
\[
F(f) = \frac{3}{2\pi} \int_{-\infty}^{\infty} \sin(2\pi t) e^{-i2\pi ft} \, dt + \frac{2}{2\pi} \int_{-\infty}^{\infty} \cos(4\pi t) e^{-i2\pi ft} \, dt
\]
\[
F(f) = \frac{3}{2\pi} \int_{-\infty}^{\infty} \sin(2\pi t) e^{-i2\pi ft} \, dt + \frac{1}{\pi} \int_{-\infty}^{\infty} \cos(4\pi t) e^{-i2\pi ft} \, dt
\]

Using the standard Fourier transform results:
1. \( \int_{-\infty}^{\infty} \sin(2\pi t) e^{-i2\pi ft} \, dt = \frac{1}{2} \left( \frac{1}{1 - 2f} - \frac{1}{1 + 2f} \right) = \frac{1}{2} \left( \frac{4f}{1 - 4f^2} \right) = \frac{2f}{1 - 4f^2} \)
2. \( \int_{-\infty}^{\infty} \cos(2\pi t) e^{-i2\pi ft} \, dt = \frac{1}{2} \left( \frac{1}{1 - f} + \frac{1}{1 + f} \right) = \frac{1}{2} \left( \frac{2}{1 - f^2} \right) = \frac{1}{1 - f^2} \)

3. \( \int_{-\infty}^{\infty} \cos(4\pi t) e^{-i2\pi ft} \, dt = \frac{1}{2} \left( \frac{1}{1 - 2f} + \frac{1}{1 + 2f} \right) = \frac{1}{2} \left( \frac{2}{1 - 4f^2} \right) = \frac{1}{1 - 4f^2} \)

Substituting these results into the expression for \( F(f) \):
\[
F(f) = \frac{3}{2\pi} \cdot \frac{2f}{1 - 4f^2} + \frac{1}{\pi} \cdot \frac{1}{1 - 4f^2}
\]
\[
F(f) = \frac{3f}{2\pi (1 - 4f^2)} + \frac{1}{\pi (1 - 4f^2)}
\]
