","The decay length of the evanescent wave is given by:
\[
\text{Decay Length} = \frac{1}{k_0 (n_{\text{eff}} - n_f)}
\]
where \(k_0\) is the free-index wavelength and \(n_{\text{eff}} - n_f\) is the difference between the refractive index of the medium and the refractive index of the surrounding medium.

For \(n_{\text{eff}} - n_f \sim 0.3\), the decay length is:
\[
\text{Decay Length} = \frac{1}{k_0 \times 0.3}
\]
For \(k_0 = \frac{2\pi}{\lambda}\), where \(\lambda = 1 \mu m = 1 \times 10^{-6} \text{ m}\):
\[
k_0 = \frac{2\pi}{1 \times 10^{-6} \text{ m}} = 6.285 \times 10^9 \text{ rad/m}
\]
Substituting \(k_0\) into the decay length formula:
\[
\text{Decay Length} = \frac{1}{6.285 \times 10^9 \text{ rad/m} \times 0.3} = \frac{1}{1.8865 \times 10^9 \text{ rad/m}} \approx 5.3 \times 10^{-10} \text{ m}
\]
Thus, the decay length is \(\boxed{5.3 \times 10^{-10} \text{ m}}\).",relevance,"# Task Description
