<USER_REQUEST>
You’re pulling a chest of drawers, applying a force of 60.0 N at an angle of 60.0°. How much work do you do pulling it over 10.0 m?
</USER_REQUEST>

<ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>
To calculate the work done, we can use the formula:

\[
W = F \cdot d \cdot \cos \theta
\]

where:
- \( W = \text{work done} \)
- \( F = \text{force applied} = 60.0 \, \text{N} \)
- \( d = \text{displacement} = 10.0 \, \text{m} \)
- \( \theta = \text{angle between force and displacement} = 60.0° \)

Substituting the values:

\[
W = 60.0 \, \text{N} \cdot 10.0 \, \text{m} \cdot \cos 60.0°
\]

Since \(\cos 60.0° = 0.5\):

\[
W = 600 \, \text{N} \cdot \text{m} \cdot 0.5 = 300 \, \text{N} \cdot \text{m}
\]

Thus, the work done is \(\boxed{300 \, \text{J}}\).
</ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>

