","To find the kinetic energy of the satellite, we first need to find its mass (m). We can do this using the gravitational force formula:

\[
F = \frac{Gm^2}{r^2}
\]

where:
- \( F = 3600 \, \text{N} \) (gravitational force)
- \( G = 8.87 \times 10^{-5} \, \text{N m}^2/\text{kg}^2 \) (gravitational constant)
- \( m_0 = 6 \times 10^{23} \, \text{kg} \) (mass of the Earth)
- \( r = 10^5 \, \text{m} \) (radius of the Earth)

Substituting the given values:

\[
3600 = \frac{8.87 \times 10^{-5} \times m_0^2}{r^2}
\]

Solving for \( m \):
\[
m = \frac{r^2 \times F}{G \times m_0}
\]

Substituting the values:
\[
m = \frac{10^5 \times 10^5 \times 3600}{8.87 \times 10^{-5} \times 6 \times 10^{23}}
\]
\[
m = \frac{3.6 \times 10^9}{5.31 \times 10^{19}}
\]
\[
m = 6.78 \times 10^{-11} \, \text{kg}
\]

The kinetic energy (KE) is given by:
\[
\text{KE} = \frac{1}{2}mv^2
\]
where:
- \( m = 6.78 \times 10^{-11} \, \text{kg} \) (mass of the satellite)
- \( v = 800 \, \text{m/s} \) (velocity of the satellite)

Substituting the values:
\[
\text{KE} = \frac{1}{2} \times 6.78 \times 10^{-11} \times (800)^2
