\]
\[
m = \frac{3.6 \times 10^9}{5.31 \times 10^{19}}
\]
\[
m = 6.78 \times 10^{-11} \, \text{kg}
\]

The kinetic energy (KE) is given by:
\[
\text{KE} = \frac{1}{2}mv^2
\]
where:
- \( m = 6.78 \times 10^{-11} \, \text{kg} \) (mass of the satellite)
- \( v = 800 \, \text{m/s} \) (velocity of the satellite)

Substituting the values:
\[
\text{KE} = \frac{1}{2} \times 6.78 \times 10^{-11} \times (800)^2
\]
\[
\text{KE} = frac{1}{2} \times 6.78 \times 10^{-11} \times 640000
\]
\[
\text{KE} = 4.31 \times 10^{-5} \, \text{J}
\]

Thus, the kinetic energy of the satellite is \(\boxed{4.31 \times 10^{-5} \, \text{J}}\).
