- \( t = 3 \) years (time in the cupot)  
- \( v = 0.6c \) (speed of the spaceship)  

First, calculate the time \( t' \) (time measured by Earth observer):  
\[
t' = \frac{3}{\sqrt{1 - (0.6c)^2/c^2}} = \frac{3}{\sqrt{1 - 0.36}} = \frac{3}{\sqrt{0.64}} = \frac{3}{0.8} = 3.75 \text{ years}
\]  
Thus, the clock in the Earth's system shows 3.75 years.  

The time dilation formula applies to identical clocks. The clock in the cargo box is also moving with the spaceship, so it will also experience time dilation relative to the Earth. The time measured by the clock in the cargo bar is the same as the time measured by the clock in the cupot, which is 3 years.  

