","The net chemical reaction for the discharge of a lead-sulfur dioxide battery is:  
\[ \text{Pb} + \text{PbSO}_4 + 2\text{H}^+ + 2e^- \rightarrow 2\text{PbSO}_4 + \text{H}_2 \]  
This reaction occurs at the anode (left side of the battery). The anode is made of lead (Pb), and the anode is in contact with a solution of sulfuric acid (H₂SO₄) and lead sulfate (PbSO₄). During discharge, the lead (Pb) reacts with the lead sulfate (PbSO₄) and hydrogen ions (H⁺) from the sulfuric acid, forming more lead sulfate (PbSO₄) and hydrogen gas (H₂).  

The other half-reaction occurs at the cathode (right side of the battery), where lead sulfate (PbSO₄) is reduced to lead (Pb) and water (H₂O):  
\[ \text{PbSO}_4 + 2e^- \rightarrow \text{Pb} + \text{SO}_4^{2-} + 2\text{H}_2\text{O} \]  

The overall reaction is the sum of these two half-reactions:  
\[ \text{Pb} + \text{PbSO}_4 + 2\text{H}^+ \rightarrow 2\text{PbSO}_4 + \text{H}_2 \]  

This reaction shows that during discharge, lead and lead sulfate are converted into lead sulfate, and hydrogen gas is released. The sulfuric acid is consumed as the battery discharges, causing the battery's voltage to decrease.  

